title: The Colfax Massacre
subtitle: Easter Sunday, Grant Parish
tags: event
new-page: true
view-if: year = 1873 and month = 4 and colfax_pending = 1
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
    Q.colfax_massacre = 1;
    Q.Union_League_strength -= 12;
    Q.Union_League_militancy += 0.05;
    Q.whiteL_strength += 4;
    Q.kkk_strength += 2;
!}

= The Colfax Massacre

On Easter Sunday, a force of armed white <span style="color: #0015bc;">Democrats</span> and White League militants surrounded the courthouse at Colfax, Louisiana. A company of Black militia had gathered there to defend Kellogg's government against supporters of John McEnery.

After a day-long siege, the courthouse was set ablaze. Those who surrendered were shot. Between sixty and more than one hundred men were killed.

News reaches Washington within days. Northern newspapers are calling it a massacre. Southern <span style="color: #0015bc;">Democrats</span> describe it as the suppression of an armed insurrection. Congress now wants to know what the federal government intends to do.

- @full_prosecution: Prosecute the perpetrators under the Enforcement Acts
- @troop_deployment: Deploy federal troops to occupy Grant Parish
- @local_authorities: Leave prosecution to Louisiana's courts
- @downplay: Downplay the massacre to limit the political fallout

@full_prosecution
choose-if: enforcement_acts = 1 and budget >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: [? if enforcement_acts = 0: Stronger legistation are required to bring federal charges of this kind. ?][? if budget < 1: We don't the budget to afford such an objective?]
on-arrival: {!
    Q.PoliticalCostPenalty += 2;
    Q.budget -= 1;
    Q.radical_strength += 3;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 4;
    Q.whiteL_militancy += 0.05;
    Q.kkk_militancy += 0.05;
    Q.Union_League_strength += 4;
    Q.cruikshank_pending = 1;
!}

Federal marshals arrest dozens of participants and bring charges under the Enforcement Acts. It is one of the first major tests of federal power against organized political violence on this scale.

[? if WhiteL_formed = 1: The <span style="color: #B5651D;">White Leagues</span> and ?] <span style="color: #0015bc;">Democrats</span> denounce the prosecutions as federal tyranny imposed on a local dispute. They vow to fight the charges in every court available to them.

@troop_deployment
choose-if: occupation >= 1 and army_loyalty >= 0.5
unavailable-subtitle: [? if occupation < 1: Federal occupation is not established enough to garrison the parish. ?][? if army_loyalty < 0.5: The Army cannot be relied on for this deployment. ?]
on-arrival: {!
    Q.budget -= 2;
    Q.Us_police_strength += 4;
    Q.Union_League_strength += 3;
    Q.whiteL_strength -= 2;
    Q.kkk_strength -= 1;
    Q.moderate_strength += 1;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 2;
!}

Federal troops move into Grant Parish to restore order and prevent further reprisals. The show of force brings a measure of calm, but no arrests follow and no one is held accountable. The troops can keep the peace. They cannot decide who will be punished.

@local_authorities
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_total_pop = Q.LA_workers + Q.LA_middle_class + Q.LA_rural + Q.LA_freemen + Q.LA_immigrant_laborers + Q.LA_unemployed;
    Q.LA_freedmen_share = Q.LA_freemen / Q.LA_total_pop;

    Q.local_justice_score = (Q.LA_reconstructionScore * 1.5)
        + (Q.LA_freedmen_share * 100)
        - ((Q.kkk_strength + Q.whiteL_strength) / 20);

    if (Q.local_justice_score >= 60) {
        Q.colfax_local_outcome = "convictions";
        Q.Union_League_strength += 6;
        Q.radical_strength += 2;
        Q.whiteL_strength += 1;
        Q.LA_reconstructionScore += 3;
    } else if (Q.local_justice_score >= 30) {
        Q.colfax_local_outcome = "mixed";
        Q.Union_League_strength -= 2;
        Q.whiteL_strength += 3;
        Q.kkk_strength += 1;
        Q.radical_dissent += 3;
    } else {
        Q.colfax_local_outcome = "acquittal";
        Q.Union_League_strength -= 6;
        Q.whiteL_strength += 5;
        Q.kkk_strength += 3;
        Q.radical_dissent += 6;
        Q.LA_reconstructionScore -= 8;
    }
!}

Washington leaves the prosecution to Louisiana's courts.

[? if colfax_local_outcome = "convictions" : Reconstruction institutions still have enough strength to secure convictions against several of those involved. It falls short of what the <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span> wanted, but the convictions send a clear warning to the **<span style="color: #B5651D;">White Leagues</span>. ?]

[? if colfax_local_outcome = "mixed" : Grand juries in Grant Parish and the surrounding countryside are divided. A handful of indictments move forward, while others are dropped or end in acquittal. Witnesses recant or refuse to appear. The <span style="color: #B5651D;">White Leagues</span> take note of how little resistance they face. ?]

[? if colfax_local_outcome = "acquittal" : Grand juries decline to indict, or acquit outright, in case after case. Louisiana's Reconstruction government is too weak and the local white majority too dominant for convictions to follow. Within weeks, it is clear that no one will face serious consequences for Colfax. The <span style="color: #B5651D;">White Leagues</span> take note. Black communities across Louisiana do as well. ?]


@downplay
choose-if: conservative_strength + liberal_strength >= 40
unavailable-subtitle: [? if conservative_strength + liberal_strength < 40: There is not enough support in the party to downplay a massacre this widely reported. ?]
on-arrival: {!
    Q.PoliticalCostPenalty -= 1;
    Q.radical_dissent += 10;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 3;
    Q.whiteL_strength += 3;
    Q.middle_class_rep -= 2;
    Q.LA_freemen_rep -= 5;
!}

Administration allies in the press describe the killings as regrettable while portraying the militia's presence at the courthouse as provocative. The account shifts attention away from the men who carried out the killings.

It limits the immediate political damage. It also draws accusations, including from within the party, that the administration would rather contain the scandal than confront it.